# 11 — Generics & Type Constraints

## What is this?
Introduced in Go 1.18, Generics allow functions and data types to declare type parameters in square brackets (`[T any]`), enabling reusable algorithms without sacrificing compile-time type safety.

## Why does Go have it?
- Replaces unsafe `interface{}` casting and repetitive boilerplate for generic algorithms (e.g. Map, Filter, Stack, Min/Max).
- Provides compile-time type verification with zero runtime reflection overhead.

## Basic Syntax
```go
// Generic function
func Min[T ~int | ~float64](a, b T) T {
    if a < b {
        return a
    }
    return b
}

// Generic struct
type Stack[T any] struct {
    items []T
}
```

## How it Behaves
- Constraints define permitted types:
  - `any`: Alias for `interface{}`.
  - `comparable`: Any type supporting `==` and `!=`.
  - `~T`: Includes type `T` and all custom types whose underlying type is `T`.

## Common Mistakes
1. Overusing generics: do not use generics when a plain interface or concrete type suffices; use generics for data structures and pure algorithms.
2. Trying to use operators (like `<`, `+`) on `any` or `comparable`: operators require specific numeric/ordered constraints.

## Small Practical Example
```go
package main

import "fmt"

func Contains[T comparable](slice []T, target T) bool {
    for _, item := range slice {
        if item == target {
            return true
        }
    }
    return false
}

func main() {
    fmt.Println(Contains([]int{1, 2, 3}, 2))
    fmt.Println(Contains([]string{"a", "b"}, "c"))
}
```

